{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~5–15 €/t), especially beyond ~day 7.",
    "Missed regime shift/mean reversion: when history is rising into the forecast start, the model extrapolates the uptrend, but the market mean-reverts and sells off; apply a downward correction when recent 10–20d momentum is strongly positive.",
    "Overshoot after local peaks: following a spike/high plateau in the last ~5–10 days of history, the model keeps prices elevated (or even higher) while outcomes decline; cap forecasts near the last observed price and impose a decay back toward a medium-term mean.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: forecast error increases with horizon; apply a progressively larger negative adjustment with lead time (small for days 1–3, moderate for days 4–10, largest for days 11–30).",
    "Insufficient drawdown speed: realized series falls faster and further than predicted during sell-offs; increase downside convexity (steeper decline) when early forecast days start drifting down or when recent volatility is high."
  ]
}
